#{[17592186262723 "![](images/fb/fbfe241043e9014c1b633d163cf7e06234db70702a9052297b5df8b9df3fd82f.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186262728 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨y+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and  $(0,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186262734 "![](images/41/414611a90c2227ff163d40a4f6f560bc67c471fa0f35724e459cec280e4904f9.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4\\,i+2\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2t}\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186262735 "![](images/85/85b4da8c0fa35867871b6254ba20f505b7596f2c97226adb2ed01339fe4cbedf.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=5{e}^{-x}\\,i+4\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,e],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186262736 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}\\,i+6x{y}^{4}\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186262737 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-3,-1),(-1,-1),(-1,1),$ and $(-3,1),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x)\\,i+(-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4})\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186262738 "![](images/9b/9bb15934c271a17851342b9032f5096f48e7ac9ee4e149af92ac1010dea46d88.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=3xy\\,i+(2x+1)\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186262739 "![](images/60/6043a1f88474e2c282938d3b3f0193002b6e11b5fb6d16292fea43d63292d1b0.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y={x}^{3}$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-3{x}^{2}\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186262740 "![](images/cd/cd21413d8eea5e2fec67eebe22967c846fd7916fd4fb0fbe5f8efe7ac9df6b1a.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=1-x$ from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186262741 "![](images/67/67dca8f0202d0c5d1041935a6aee7c4ebd0e3c6c449f2ee1a5ff24c1f381410c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186262752 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x,\\,{x}^{5}-3y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186262753 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3},\\,x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186262754 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y},\\,3{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=2.$"] [17592186262755 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x-\\sqrt{y},\\,y-\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [17592186262757 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\sqrt[3]{y}+x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(0,1).$"] [17592186262758 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}+2y,\\,-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,1),$ and $(-2,1).$"] [17592186262759 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$"] [17592186262760 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,{x}^{3}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$"] [17592186262762 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨30xy,\\,15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y,$ $\\,x=-\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$"] [17592186262772 "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ from $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$"] [17592186262773 "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1?$"] [17592186262794 "![](images/16/162c0d0310cbbb533c30af373820751fc6c5a447127958486571224d7f8ac07a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [17592186262798 "![](images/c4/c4aa91668440687bbae4b1f85672f5cbaf7ce00d978049c7558eed99b7361d1a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨3t-{t}^{3},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1.$"] [17592186262801 "![](images/33/33412629fc30584cf4a1726eef7e4f23f623ae4439d25045d61e36f68914dacd.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{1}{3}({t}^{2}+2)^{3/2}⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=2.$"] [17592186262810 "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=0.$"] [17592186262873 "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨-1,1,0⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨6,1,7⟩.$"] [17592186262874 "Let $a=⟨0,9,3⟩,$ $b=⟨3,2,0⟩$ and $c=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c).$"] [17592186262883 "Let $p=⟨7,4,9⟩,$ $q=⟨1,2,-2⟩$ and $r=⟨3,0,5⟩.$ Find $q\\cdot (r\\times p).$"] [17592186262884 "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b),$ if $a\\cdot ((-3b)\\times (-7c))=7.$"] [17592186262885 "Find $b\\cdot (a\\times c),$ if $b\\times c=⟨2,5,1⟩$ and $a=⟨6,1,-9⟩.$"] [17592186262886 "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a),$ if $b\\times c=⟨6,-1,3⟩$ and $a=⟨3,5,-4⟩.$"] [17592186266054 "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle F=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,\n$$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$"] [17592186266055 "Given that $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,i+xy\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$"] [17592186266056 "Consider the vector field $F=2xy{z}^{2}\\,i+x{e}^{y}\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$"] [17592186266057 "Consider the vector field $F=xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+y{x}^{2}\\,j+x{z}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,0,-2).$"] [17592186266058 "Consider the vector field $F=x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+xy{e}^{z}\\,j+z(x+1)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,1,0).$"] [17592186266075 "Given that $F(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}\\,i+yz\\,j+{e}^{yz}\\,k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$"] [17592186266076 "Given that $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$"] [17592186266078 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0).$"] [17592186269204 "Let $a=2i-3j+5k$ and $b=-i+4j+3k.$ Determine $a\\times b.$"] [17592186269253 "Let $a=4i+2j-k$ and $b=2i+2j-k.$ Find $a\\times b.$"] [17592186271969 "Let $a=8i-2j+4k$ and $b=6i+4j-\\frac{1}{2}k.$ Find $a\\times b.$"] [17592186272079 "Let $u=7i+3j-4k$ and $v=-i-4j+3k.$ Find $u\\times v.$"] [17592186272141 "Let $u=3i-2k$ and $v=-2i+j+5k.$ Determine $u\\times v.$"] [17592186272146 "Let $a=⟨0,1,6⟩$ and $b=⟨13,0,0⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$"] [17592186272170 "Let $a=⟨-3,5,9⟩$ and $b=⟨2,8,-4⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$"] [17592186275327 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y={cos}^{2}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"] [17592186278369 "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186278495 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=3x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 5$ and $-3\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186278506 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186278509 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$"] [17592186278510 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186278512 "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186278515 "For a surface $S$ given by $y=-{x}^{2}z$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,xz+Q+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*"] [17592186278516 "For a surface $S$ given by $x=2yz$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,z+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186278518 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=y-x$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186278519 "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}-R\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186278522 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$"] [17592186278545 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186278546 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$"] [17592186278547 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2\\sqrt{xy}$ for $3\\le x\\le 4$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$"] [17592186278548 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186278551 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186281499 "![](images/58/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨x-y,\\,y+z,\\,2x-3z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$"] [17592186281500 "![](images/aa/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x+y{e}^{z},\\,xy+2,\\,1-xz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$"] [17592186281501 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=4$ and the vector field $F=z\\,i+6y\\,j+(x+3z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186281502 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the vector field $F=({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,i+4y\\,j+2xz\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186281503 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z={x}^{2}+y.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(x+y)\\,i-2y\\,j+{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$"] [17592186281504 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and $y=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({y}^{3}-z)\\,i+z\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$"] [17592186281505 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=4,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}y\\,i+2z\\,j+xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186281506 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=e,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-{y}^{3})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186281507 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+(x+{y}^{2})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186281508 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2y\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186287896 "Let $a=⟨-2,0,1⟩$, $b=⟨5,2,-4⟩$ and $c=⟨1,0,3⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c).$"] [17592186287897 "Find $b\\cdot (a\\times c)$, if $4a\\cdot (-b\\times c)=12.$"] [17592186287957 "Let $a=⟨2,3,0⟩$ and $b=⟨6,4,0⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$"] [17592186287958 "Let $a=⟨0,0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,0,1⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$"] [17592186287959 "Let $a=⟨x,2,3⟩$, $b=⟨2x,5,2⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{393}.$ Find $x.$"] [17592186287960 "Let $a=⟨1,5,4⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3,0⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$"] [17592186287961 "Let $a=⟨-2,2,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$"]}
